P1. Claim 1: 'core local identity' T((A+1)(B+1)-1)-T(AB-1) = T(A)+T(B)+Shear(A,B), Shear = A(B^2-1)+B(A^2-1); horizontal/vertical flows of S(A,B)
==============================================================================
(i) stated identity, (A,B) in [1,7]^2: 49 of 49 pairs FAIL
    witness A=B=1: LHS = T(3)-T(0) = 6, RHS = 2
(ii) exact polynomials:
    LHS  = T(AB+A+B)-T(AB-1) = A^2B + AB^2 + 1/2*A^2 + 2*AB + 1/2*B^2 + 1/2*A + 1/2*B
    RHS  = T(A)+T(B)+A(B^2-1)+B(A^2-1) = A^2B + AB^2 + 1/2*A^2 + 1/2*B^2 - 1/2*A - 1/2*B
    LHS-RHS = 2*AB + A + B  = A(B+1)+B(A+1)
    PROVED: T((A+1)(B+1)-1)-T(AB-1) = sum_{k=AB}^{AB+A+B} k = (A+B+1)(2AB+A+B)/2
                                  = T(A)+T(B)+AB(A+B+2) = T(A)+T(B)+2A*T(B)+2B*T(A).
(iii) shifted readings T(N+s1)-T(AB+s2) = T(A+t1)+T(B+t2)+Shear valid on [0,7]^2, shifts in [-3,3]:
    solutions (s1,s2,t1,t2): [(-3, -2, -2, -2), (-1, 0, 0, 0)]
    PROVED (polynomial identity): T((A+1)(B+1)-2) - T(AB) = T(A)+T(B)+A(B^2-1)+B(A^2-1)
      i.e. the paste is off by one in BOTH triangular arguments; the second solution is its
      reflection under T(n-1)=T(-n).  Witness of the repair: A=B=1: T(2)-T(1)=2 = T(1)+T(1)+0.
    PROVED (coefficient comparison): for ANY quadratic T(n)=a n^2+b n+c the stated form forces a=1/2
      from the A^2B term and then leaves an uncancelled 2AB on the left; so no quadratic reading works.
(iv) flows Delta_A S = A+B^2+2AB+B, Delta_B S = B+A^2+2AB+A are compatible (mixed differences both 2A+2B+2)
    and their UNIQUE solution up to the constant S(0,0) is
      S(A,B) = AB(A+B) + C(A,2) + C(B,2) = T(A)+T(B)+A(B^2-1)+B(A^2-1)  (the paste's RIGHT side),
      = T((A+1)(B+1)-2) - T(AB).  The paste's LEFT side T((A+1)(B+1)-1)-T(AB-1) has horizontal flow
      Delta_A = (A+B^2+2AB+B) + (2B+1), so it is NOT the S of the flows.  Verdict: TRUE-with-repair.
    uniqueness: two solutions of both difference equations differ by a function constant in A and in B.

==============================================================================
P2. Claim 2: tetrahedral and pentatope laws; general d-simplex convolution law
==============================================================================
(i) FINITE-EXACT [0,11]^2: tetrahedral law holds (0 failures), pentatope law holds (0 failures).
    the 2D member of the same family, T(A+B+1) = T(A)+T(B)+(A+1)(B+1), also holds (this is the
    correct 'locking' identity that Claim 1 garbled).
(ii) PROVED (Chu-Vandermonde): for every d>=1 and every split a+b=d,
      S_d(A+B+1) = C(A+B+d,d) = sum_{i=0}^{d} C(A+a,i) C(B+b,d-i),  S_d(n):=C(n+d-1,d).
    Proof: choose a d-subset of a set of A+B+d = (A+a)+(B+b) elements by how many lie in the first block.
    Tetrahedral law = split (a,b)=(2,1) rewritten with Pascal: C(B+1,3)+(A+2)C(B+1,2) = Te(B)+(A+1)T(B),
      C(A+2,2)(B+1) = (B+1)T(A)+(A+1)(B+1), C(A+2,3) = Te(A).
    Pentatope law = split (2,2): C(B+2,4)+(A+2)C(B+2,3) = Pt(B)+(A+1)Te(B) (and symmetrically),
      middle term C(A+2,2)C(B+2,2) = T(A+1)T(B+1).
(iii) PROVED (the identity sum_k C(x+k,k)C(y+d-k,d-k) = C(x+y+d+1,d), x=A, y=B-1): the simplex convolution
      S_d(A+B+1) = sum_{i+j=d} S_i(A+1) S_j(B), checked exactly for d<=8 on [0,11]^2.
    Physical content that survives: a convolution (set-partition count) identity; there is no shear,
    rotation, or 'locking plate' -- every term is C(x,i)C(y,d-i) for a two-block split.

==============================================================================
P3. Claim 3: the g-operator
==============================================================================
(i) 'AgB = AB*(AgB)': as an equation in the unknown AgB it forces (AB-1)*(AgB)=0, so AgB=0 unless AB=1:
    circular / vacuous.  REFUTED as a definition.
(ii) '(AB)^(AB)' at A=1 gives B^B; '1gB=B!' gives B!; B^B != B! for B>=2: witnesses [(2, 4, 2), (3, 27, 6), (4, 256, 24)]
    REFUTED (mutually inconsistent readings; minimal witness B=2: 4 vs 2).
(iii) PROVED: the reading AgB := A!*B! satisfies the (presumably intended) recursion AgB = AB*((A-1)g(B-1)),
      1gB = B!, and AgA = (A!)^2 simultaneously.  Any solution f of f(A,B)=AB f(A-1,B-1) with f(1,B)=B!
      has f(0,k)=k! and f(A,A)=(A!)^2 f(0,0)=(A!)^2, so A!B! is the unique symmetric solution.
      The alternative AgB := A^B B! (recursion AgB = AB*(Ag(B-1))) gives 1gB=B! but AgA = A^A A! != (A!)^2
      (A=2: 8 vs 4).  '(AB)^(AB)' satisfies neither recursion.
(iv) 'squares the information capacity, mapping the Pell and square-triangular families': SCOPE, no map found
      ((A!)^2 is a square by construction; square-triangular numbers T(m)=k^2 are the Pell equation
      (2m+1)^2-8k^2=1 and involve no factorials).

==============================================================================
P4. Claim 4: tournament 'compression' T_{n-2}; Redei; counting
==============================================================================
(i) TRUE (arithmetic): C(n,2)-(n-1) = C(n-1,2) = T(n-2) for all n>=2 (checked n<=12; identity C(n,2)=C(n-1,2)+(n-1)).
(ii) labelled tournaments, exact census:
      n  #T=2^C(n,2)  sum_T h(T)  n!*2^T(n-2)  min h  max h  all h odd  h-values seen
      2           2           2            2      1      1  True  [1]
      3           8          12           12      1      3  True  [1, 3]
      4          64         192          192      1      5  True  [1, 3, 5]
      5        1024        7680         7680      1     15  True  [1, 3, 5, 9, 11, 13, 15]
      6       32768      737280       737280      1     45  True  [1, 3, 5, 9, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19, 23, 25, 27, 29, 31, 33, 37, 41, 43, 45]
    PROVED (double counting): every ordering pi of [n] is the Hamiltonian path of exactly 2^{T(n-2)} labelled
      tournaments (the n-1 path edges are forced, the other T(n-2) are free), so sum_T h(T) = n! 2^{T(n-2)}
      and the average number of Hamiltonian paths is n!/2^{n-1} (n=6: 45/2).
(iii) REFUTED ('path edges carry 0 bits'): the code (pi, T(n-2) free bits) has n!*2^{T(n-2)} codewords for
      2^{C(n,2)} tournaments; it is surjective and (n!/2^{n-1})-to-one on average, so it costs
      log2(n!) + T(n-2) = C(n,2) + log2(n!/2^{n-1}) >= C(n,2) bits, with strict excess for n>=3.
        n= 3: C(n,2)=  3, T(n-2)=  1, log2(n!)=2.585, excess over C(n,2) = 0.585 bits
        n= 4: C(n,2)=  6, T(n-2)=  3, log2(n!)=4.585, excess over C(n,2) = 1.585 bits
        n= 5: C(n,2)= 10, T(n-2)=  6, log2(n!)=6.907, excess over C(n,2) = 2.907 bits
        n= 6: C(n,2)= 15, T(n-2)= 10, log2(n!)=9.492, excess over C(n,2) = 4.492 bits
        n= 8: C(n,2)= 28, T(n-2)= 21, log2(n!)=15.299, excess over C(n,2) = 8.299 bits
        n=10: C(n,2)= 45, T(n-2)= 36, log2(n!)=21.791, excess over C(n,2) = 12.791 bits
      The uniform distribution on labelled tournaments has entropy exactly C(n,2) bits; no lossless code
      beats it (Shannon source coding, CITED: Shannon 1948).  What IS true: h is odd (Redei 1934, CITED),
      h-spectrum = odds minus {7,21} and |Aut(T)| divides h (01-canon/theorems/THM-1745-leaf-graded-...).
(iv) 'defects at diffraction peaks of the quasicrystalline Wythoff wave', 'topological genus of randomness':
      SCOPE -- no defined object, no map found.

