P5. Claim 5a: horizons and the '63 bottleneck' (Bang / Catalan)
==============================================================================
(i) 'horizon lines' (Z,2Z),(Z,Z^2),(W,W^3),(W,W^4) are the diagonals X=Y of X+Y, X*Y, X*Y*W... : definitions only (SCOPE).
(ii) 63 = 3*7*3 = 3^2*7 = 2^6-1 = (2^3-1)(2^3+1) = 7*9; 9 = 3^2 = 2^3+1 (Catalan 3^2-2^3=1, Mihailescu 2004 CITED).
    2^n-1, 1<=n<=40: indices with NO primitive prime divisor = [1, 6]
    PROVED (Bang 1886 / Zsigmondy 1892, CITED): the ONLY n>1 for which 2^n-1 lacks a primitive prime is n=6, i.e. 63.
    So the paste's '63 short-circuits Bang's theorem' is TRUE-with-repair: the mechanism is 2^3+1 = 3^2 (a prime
    power already dividing 2^2-1), not a 'loop switching a multiplication edge and an addition edge'; no such
    loop is defined.  Inherited: wave-one zsigmondy lane, 05-knowledge/results/collatz_mod6_20260917_zsigmondy_triad.out
    (Catalan reading of n=6; no prime p has ord_p(2)=6).
    ord_9(2) = 6 and 9 | 63; no prime has multiplicative order 6 to base 2 (primes of order 6 would be primitive).

