P6. Claim 5b: the '341 bottleneck' (Zsigmondy for 4^n-1; Cipolla pseudoprimes; the R-trunk)
==============================================================================
(i) 341 = 11*31 = (4^5-1)/3 = 4^4+4^3+4^2+4+1 (base-4 repunit): TRUE arithmetic.
    4^5-1 = 1023 = {3: 1, 11: 1, 31: 1}; primitive primes (ord_p(4)=5): [11, 31]
    4^n-1, 1<=n<=40: primitive prime divisors
      n= 1  4^n-1 = {3: 1}  primitive: [3]
      n= 2  4^n-1 = {3: 1, 5: 1}  primitive: [5]
      n= 3  4^n-1 = {3: 2, 7: 1}  primitive: [7]
      n= 4  4^n-1 = {3: 1, 5: 1, 17: 1}  primitive: [17]
      n= 5  4^n-1 = {3: 1, 11: 1, 31: 1}  primitive: [11, 31]
      n= 6  4^n-1 = {3: 2, 5: 1, 7: 1, 13: 1}  primitive: [13]
      n= 7  4^n-1 = {3: 1, 43: 1, 127: 1}  primitive: [43, 127]
      n= 8  4^n-1 = {3: 1, 5: 1, 17: 1, 257: 1}  primitive: [257]
      n= 9  4^n-1 = {3: 3, 7: 1, 19: 1, 73: 1}  primitive: [19, 73]
      n=10  4^n-1 = {3: 1, 5: 2, 11: 1, 31: 1, 41: 1}  primitive: [41]
      n=11  4^n-1 = {3: 1, 23: 1, 89: 1, 683: 1}  primitive: [23, 89, 683]
      n=12  4^n-1 = {3: 2, 5: 1, 7: 1, 13: 1, 17: 1, 241: 1}  primitive: [241]
      n=20  4^n-1 = {3: 1, 5: 2, 11: 1, 17: 1, 31: 1, 41: 1, 61681: 1}  primitive: [61681]
      n=30  4^n-1 = {3: 2, 5: 2, 7: 1, 11: 1, 13: 1, 31: 1, 41: 1, 61: 1, 151: 1, 331: 1, 1321: 1}  primitive: [61, 1321]
      n=40  4^n-1 = {3: 1, 5: 2, 11: 1, 17: 1, 31: 1, 41: 1, 257: 1, 61681: 1, 4278255361: 1}  primitive: [4278255361]
    indices without a primitive prime: []
    PROVED (Zsigmondy, CITED; exceptions are only n=1 with a-b=1, n=2 with a+b a power of 2, and (2,1,6)):
      4-1=3 != 1 and 4+1=5 is not a power of 2, so 4^n-1 has a primitive prime divisor for EVERY n>=1.
    REFUTED: '341 stalls primitive prime generation of the 4x+1 sequence' -- minimal witness: 4^5-1 has
      two primitive primes 11 and 31, both dividing 341.
(ii) Cipolla (1904, CITED): for prime p>=5, N_p=(4^p-1)/3 is a composite base-2 Fermat pseudoprime.
      p   N_p            composite  2^(N-1) mod N  factorisation
       5  341             True  1  {11: 1, 31: 1}
       7  5461            True  1  {43: 1, 127: 1}
      11  1398101         True  1  {23: 1, 89: 1, 683: 1}
      13  22369621        True  1  {2731: 1, 8191: 1}
      17  5726623061      True  1  {131071: 1, 43691: 1}
      19  91625968981     True  1  {174763: 1, 524287: 1}
      23  23456248059221  True  1  {47: 1, 178481: 1, 2796203: 1}
    Proof sketch: N=(2^p-1)(2^p+1)/3 is composite for p>=5 (both factors >3, and 3 divides exactly one of them);
      N-1 = 4(4^{p-1}-1)/3 is divisible by 2p (p | 4^{p-1}-1 by Fermat, and 2 | it), and ord_N(2) divides 2p
      since 2^{2p} = 4^p = 3N+1 = 1 mod N; hence 2^{N-1} = 1 mod N.
(iii) R(n)=4n+1 orbit of 0: [0, 1, 5, 21, 85, 341, 1365] = (4^j-1)/3, j=0..6
      3*((4^j-1)/3)+1 = 4^j: these are exactly the odd predecessors of the powers of two (the trunk of 1),
      the inverse-fibre braid n -> R(n) of 05-knowledge/results/arithmetic_braids_20260917_collatz.md (B1)-(B2).
      341 = R^5(0) = R^4(1) is on the trunk; 'zero-friction state in G_4' has no defined content (SCOPE).

